.\" usbscope(1) — manual page for the usbscope CLI
.\" Kept in sync by hand with `usbscope --help` (Sources/usbscope/main.swift);
.\" update it when the interface changes.
.TH USBSCOPE 1 "2026-10-04" "usbscope 0.9.0" "usbscope manual"
.SH NAME
usbscope \- inspect USB buses, USB-C ports, cables and negotiated link modes on macOS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B usbscope
.RI [ view ]
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-color ]
.RB [ \-\-watch
.IR seconds ]
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.PP
.B usbscope check
.RB [ \-\-expect
.IR expr ]
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.PP
.B usbscope watch \-\-events
.RB [ \-\-interval
.IR seconds ]
.PP
.B usbscope baseline
.RI save | check
.IR file
.PP
.B usbscope report
.RB [ \-\-format
.IR md | html ]
.RB [ \-\-out
.IR file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B usbscope
renders what macOS already knows about the machine's USB subsystem: the
receptacles of the hardware port manager, the negotiated USB modes, the attached
devices and the cable/port-controller facts the system publishes. The
.B IOPort
registry plane is read in\-process through IOKit by default, with
.BR ioreg (8)
kept as a fallback; the other sources are
.BR system_profiler (8)
and the
.B AppleSmartBattery
registry node. It never needs sudo, private frameworks or entitlements.
.PP
The command is a single Swift program
.RB ( Sources/usbscope/main.swift );
there is no second implementation.
.SH VIEWS
.TP
.B overview
summary panel, port table, device tree and USB4 table (the default).
.TP
.B ports
USB-C / Thunderbolt port table with the negotiated mode per receptacle.
.TP
.B devices
bus to device tree with vendor/product IDs, link mode, serial and port mapping.
.TP
.B cables
cable class (passive / e\-marked / active / optical), CC authentication and hash
status, SOP specification revision and LDCM liquid status.
.TP
.B thunderbolt
Thunderbolt / USB4 receptacles with state and link speed.
.TP
.B usb4
the USB4 fabric as a tree: routers, their ports with the raw link speed and width
enumerations, and the PCIe/USB/DisplayPort tunnels on them. The enumerations are
printed verbatim because macOS does not document their encoding.
.TP
.B security
security findings per device/port (severity plus a short reason) and the USB
mass\-storage inventory.
.TP
.B json
machine\-readable snapshot; identical to
.BR \-\-json .
.SH COMMANDS
Four commands turn the inspector into something a script can use.
.TP
.B check \-\-expect
assert facts about the current machine and set the exit code:
.B 0
when every expectation holds,
.B 3
when one fails,
.B 2
on a malformed expectation. Repeat
.B \-\-expect
for several assertions; the forms are
.IR device=vid:pid|name ,
.IR port=name ,
.IR connected>=N ,
.IR devices>=N
and
.IR warning=0 .
.TP
.B watch \-\-events
print one flushed JSON line per attach/detach
.RI ( kind ,
.IR timestamp ,
name, VID/PID, serial, location id, port) so it can be piped. It is driven by
IOKit notifications and falls back to a polling differ;
.B \-\-interval
sets that poll interval (default 2 seconds).
.TP
.B baseline save \fIfile\fR | baseline check \fIfile\fR
store a snapshot as the known\-good baseline or compare the live machine against
it: appeared, disappeared and changed ports; exit
.B 0
when identical,
.B 3
when different and
.B 2
when the baseline cannot be read.
.TP
.B report
print a human\-readable report (machine, ports, devices with class/tier, cables,
power contract, security findings, warnings). Markdown by default; a
self\-contained styled HTML page with
.BR \-\-format\ html ;
.B \-\-out
writes it to a file instead of stdout.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-v \", \" \-\-verbose
show additional details: raw controller enumerations, plug orientation, pin
assignment, power contract, per\-transport rate and signaling, TRM state, and the
system\-wide charging panel in the
.B cables
view.
.TP
.B \-\-json
print the snapshot as JSON and exit. Combined with
.B \-\-watch
it prints one snapshot instead, because JSON in a live loop makes no sense.
.TP
.B \-\-progress
name each source as it is read (ports, buses, thunderbolt, hardware, charging,
registry) on standard error, so a collect that takes over a second is not a silent
wait. Standard output stays clean, which keeps
.B \-\-json
parseable.
.TP
.BI \-\-watch " seconds"
accepted for periodic refresh; the current CLI redraws the selected view in place
until interrupted with Ctrl-C, appending frames instead of clearing the screen
when stdout is a pipe or a file. With the JSON option it prints a single snapshot
instead, because a stream cannot be read as one JSON document.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
disable colours (also honoured when stdout is not a terminal).
.TP
.BR \-h \", \" \-\-help
print usage and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
print the version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success, or
.B check
/
.B baseline
found no difference.
.TP
.B 1
unexpected failure (a report that cannot be written, a baseline that cannot be
saved; a missing data source is a warning, not a failure).
.TP
.B 2
usage or parse error, a malformed expectation, or a baseline that cannot be read.
.TP
.B 3
.B check
or
.B baseline
found a mismatch.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B usbscope
the overview of every view.
.TP
.B usbscope ports \-v
the port table with the raw per\-transport detail.
.TP
.B usbscope cables
cable, e\-marker and CC authentication information.
.TP
.B usbscope check \-\-expect 'connected>=1'
assert at least one connected receptacle; exits 0 or 3.
.TP
.B usbscope baseline save ~/known\-good.json
store the current machine as a baseline.
.TP
.B usbscope report \-\-format html \-\-out report.html
a self\-contained HTML report on disk.
.TP
.B usbscope watch \-\-events | jq \-c .
one JSON event per attach/detach, piped to
.BR jq (1).
.TP
.B usbscope \-\-json | jq .summary
the machine summary as JSON.
.TP
.B usbscope devices \-\-watch 2
the device tree, refreshed every two seconds.
.SH DATA SOURCES
.TP
.B system_profiler SPUSBHostDataType \-json
bus tree, devices, link speed, VID/PID and serial.
.TP
.B system_profiler SPUSBDataType \-json
fallback for machines that only expose the legacy device list.
.TP
.B system_profiler SPThunderboltDataType \-json
Thunderbolt / USB4 receptacles.
.TP
.B system_profiler SPPowerDataType \-json
adapter watt, charger state, state of charge.
.TP
.B ioreg \-a \-l \-w0 \-p IOPort
port controller: ports, transports, cable/CC/SOP data, power contract, LDCM, TRM
(read in\-process through IOKit by default).
.TP
.B ioreg \-a \-l \-w0 \-p IOUSB
USB device tree: descriptors, enumeration speed code, hub tier.
.TP
.B ioreg \-r \-n AppleSmartBattery \-a \-l \-w0
live power telemetry (adapter input, system load, battery flow).
.PP
Every source is optional at runtime: a failing source adds a warning that is shown
in a notes panel (or in the
.B warnings
array of the JSON) and never aborts the run.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/Library/Preferences/com.zopyx.usbscope.plist
preferences of the native app (view, cadence, sort state, window frame).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ioreg (8),
.BR system_profiler (8),
.BR jq (1)
.PP
The JSON schema is documented in
.IR docs/index.md ;
the signing, notarisation and App Store status in
.IR docs/distribution.md
and
.IR docs/app-store.md .
.SH NOTES
The app bundle built by
.I scripts/build-swift-app.sh
is signed
.B ad hoc
.RB ( "codesign \-s \-" ),
not with a Developer ID and not notarised, so a download is still stopped by
Gatekeeper until it is opened once from the Finder or the quarantine flag is
removed. No prebuilt binary is published yet. See
.IR docs/distribution.md .
